\import{macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{frap}

\refcardt{Definition}{Abstract polytopes and flags}{Section 2}{schulte2017regular}{
  \p{An \newvocab{abstract #{n}-polytope}, for #{n\geq0}, is a
  \vocabk{partially ordered set}{tt-002C} #{P} of \newvocab{faces} with a least face
  #{F_{-1}} and greatest face #{F_n}. Every chain extends to a maximal chain,
  called a \newvocab{flag}, with exactly #{n+2} faces. Faces in each flag
  have \newvocab{ranks} #{-1,0,\ldots,n}, consistently
  across flags. Faces other than the least and greatest are \em{proper};
  vertices, edges and facets have ranks #{0,1,n-1}.}
  \p{The \newvocab{diamond condition} says that between faces #{F<G} of
  ranks #{i-1} and #{i+1} there are exactly two faces of rank #{i},
  for #{0\leq i<n}.
  Two flags are \em{adjacent} if they differ in exactly one proper face.
  \newvocab{Strong flag connectivity} requires any two flags to be joined
  by adjacent flags, each containing their intersection.}
  \p{The diamond condition gives a unique rank-#{i} neighbor of each flag.
  Connectivity concerns flags, not arbitrary paths through the least face:
  such paths would connect every pair without imposing an axiom.
  These conditions prescribe incidence without choosing lengths or angles.}
  \p{A rank-zero polytope is a two-element chain. A rank-one polytope
  has two vertices between its least and greatest faces. These low-rank
  cases occur as [sections](frap-000E) of higher-rank polytopes.}
}
